<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arab League</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as League of Arab States</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arab League is a regional organisation of Arab states founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> on 22 March 1945 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, Transjordan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> joining weeks later. It grew to 22 members across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, and works to coordinate the policies of its members, though often divided. In 1961 its troops replaced British forces in Kuwait to deter an Iraqi invasion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arab-league.jpg" alt="Arab League" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flad · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Arab_League.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It belongs to the <b>Arab League</b>, the Arab Maghreb Union and the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation; it left the Organisation of African Unity in 1984 when the Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic was admitted and rejoined the African Union in 2017.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It is a founding member of the United Nations and the <b>Arab League</b>, held an Arab League summit in 2002, concluded an association agreement with the European Union in 2001 and sits in the Non-Aligned Movement, the Organization of Islamic Cooperation and the Group of 77.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lebanese-republic">Lebanese Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>On 29 November 1947 the General Assembly adopted Resolution 181 by 33 to 13, the United States and the Soviet Union in favour and the <b>Arab League</b> members against.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>It was a founding member of the United Nations, the Non-Aligned Movement, the <b>Arab League</b>, the African Union and the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, and is a member of BRICS.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-egypt">Republic of Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It is a founding member of the <b>Arab League</b>, the Gulf Cooperation Council, the Muslim World League and the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation and the only Arab state in the G20.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Sheikh Saqr bin Sultan (r. 1951–1965) sympathised with Arab nationalism and welcomed contacts with the <b>Arab League</b>, and in 1965 the British, who regarded his policies as a threat, had him deposed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sheikhdom-of-sharjah">Sheikhdom of Sharjah · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
